==Latin==

===Etymology 1===
Pokorny<ref>{{R:ine:IEW|pages=36-37|head=am(m)a}}</ref> suggests a derivation from {{der|la|ine-pro|*amma-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ama-|t=mother}}. Compare {{cog|la|amita|t=paternal aunt}}, {{cog|la|anus|t=crone}} and {{cog|goh|amma|t=wet nurse}}.

It may also represent an infantile modification of {{m|la|mamma}}.

====Noun====
{{la-noun|amma<1>}} {{tlb|la|LL.}}

# a [[nocturnal]] bird, perhaps the [[screech owl]]
#* {{quote-text|la|year=c. 600|author=Isidore of Seville|title=Etymologiae|section=XII.vii.42
|passage=Haec avis [viz. {{m|la|strix}}] vulgo '''amma''' dicitur, ab amando parvulos; unde et lac praebere fertur nascentibus.
|translation=This bird is commonly called ''amma'' [mum [[(?)]]], because it loves its young, and it is also said to offer milk to its hatchlings.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Based on etymological evidence and the reference to milk in the Isidore quote, it is suspected that this word actually meant “mother”, along similar lines to {{m|la|atta|t=father}}.

=====Declension=====
{{la-ndecl|amma<1>}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|ca|ama}}
* {{desc|gl|ama}}
* {{desc|pt|ama}}
* {{desc|es|ama}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|la|syc|ܐܸܡܵܐ|t=mother, abbess|tr=ʾemmā}} via {{der|la|grc-koi|ἀμμᾶς}}, {{m|grc|ἀμμὰ}}, perhaps reinforced by Etymology 1.

====Noun====
{{la-noun|amma<1>}} {{tlb|la|LL.}}

